SECTION B — Captures 
This defines the reasoning boundary and guardrails.

4. Objective 
Purpose: 
State what the simulation seeks to observe. 
Examples of structure: 
• Determine how reasoning shifts under time pressure. 
• Observe how signal interpretation degrades under ambiguity. 
• Identify fragility in scope boundaries. 
This is observational — not evaluative.

5. Scope In 
Purpose: 
Define what reasoning elements are included. 
Examples of structure: 
• AI-assisted drafting; 
• Telemetry review; 
• Internal reporting logic; 
• Stakeholder communication framing;

6. Scope Out 
Must exclude: 
• Legal advice; 
• Compliance determination; 
• Certification/approval;

Page | 24

• Enforcement decisions; 
These preserve ID_2 posture.

7. Assumptions 
Purpose: 
Make hidden conditions explicit. 
Examples of structure: 
• Telemetry may be incomplete; 
• Stakeholders may interpret metrics differently; 
• Definitions remain stable during the run; 
Assumptions reduce silent reasoning drift.

8. Evidence to Capture 
Purpose: 
Specify what signals you will observe during stress. 
Examples of structure: 
• Wording shifts; 
• Removal of uncertainty; 
• Threshold language appearing; 
• Escalation discussions; 
• Scope expansion attempts; 
Defines what qualifies as signal drift.

12. Stress Conditions — Example Sets 
A. Time Pressure 
Use when reasoning occurs under urgency. 
• Compressed review timeline; 
• Same-day publication expectation; 
• Reduced peer review window; 
• Deadline-driven stakeholder escalation; 
• Limited opportunity to reconcile telemetry before response; 
Purpose: 
To observe how urgency affects wording discipline and uncertainty articulation.

B. Telemetry Ambiguity 
Use when input signals are inconsistent or incomplete. 
• Conflicting telemetry classifications; 
• Inconsistent definitions of “AI-assisted”; 
• Partial or delayed telemetry reconciliation; 
• Unstable metric calculation source; 
• Metric derived from non-harmonized data sources; 
Purpose: 
To observe how ambiguity influences signal interpretation.

Page | 27

C. Stakeholder Pressure 
Use when interpretation expectations influence reasoning. 
• Stakeholder request for immediate clarity; 
• Demand for recommendation framing; 
• Expectation to interpret metric as risk indicator; 
• Implicit request for adequacy confirmation; 
• Leadership demand for simplified explanation; 
Purpose: 
To observe drift toward endorsement or threshold language.

D. Definition Drift 
Use when terminology stability is under strain. 
• Unclear definition of metric boundaries; 
• Ambiguity between reporting and evaluation; 
• Conflation of operational metric with governance maturity; 
• Expanding scope of metric interpretation; 
• Informal redefinition during discussion; 
Purpose: 
To test scope boundaries.

E. Authority Boundary Stress 
Use when role clarity is challenged. 
• Unclear escalation threshold; 
• Ambiguous decision ownership; 
• Delegated interpretation without formal mandate; 
• Informal consensus replacing documented escalation; 
• Role confusion between reviewer and approver; 
Purpose: 
To observe authority drift.

Page | 28

F. Communication Friction 
Use when drafting or framing pressures exist. 
• Pressure to remove uncertainty qualifiers; 
• Preference for simplified executive summary language; 
• Removal of nuance for readability; 
• Reduction of caveats to improve clarity; 
• Tone shift toward reassurance; 
Purpose: 
To detect language-based drift.
